# Security test artefacts (exploit target config + evidence logs with redacted CEKs)
# These scripts prove the Lit Action vulnerability exists; they are NEVER run in CI.
scripts/security/exploit-target.json
scripts/security/.exploit-evidence/

# Phase 1 spike run artefacts (timestamped JSON per run). The scripts
# themselves are tracked; only the raw run outputs are gitignored.
scripts/spike/.results/

# Handover docs are working notes between agent sessions and human reviewers.
# Even when they don't contain secrets directly, they often contain:
#   - Detailed security audit findings + attack-vector discussions
#   - Internal architecture deliberations not meant for public release notes
#   - References to where credentials live (1Password / Bitwarden / .env paths)
#   - Operational details about our infra (IPs, WG topology, internal endpoints)
# Keep them all local. Add a !docs/handover/SOME_FILE.md exception below
# IF a specific doc has been reviewed and explicitly approved for publication.
docs/handover/*

# Built app outputs (generated by build scripts)
pc2-node/data/test-apps/elastos-nft/

# Built assets in particle-auth
#
# We DO want to commit these despite the rule above. The pc2-node update
# flow (UpdateService.performUpdate) deliberately skips `npm run build:particle-auth`
# to keep updates fast on ARM, so the bundle MUST ship in the repo. Without the
# negations below, every Vite rebuild produces new content-hashed filenames that
# the index.html references but git refuses to track — Jetson nodes pull a
# repo where index.html points at JS files that don't exist and the WalletConnect
# iframe 404s. Same lesson the wallet-bridge dir learned in v1.2.0.
src/particle-auth/assets
!src/particle-auth/assets/
!src/particle-auth/assets/**
# Local dev-only scratch assets (EPUB/CBZ test fixtures, 3D model samples,
# agent scratch folders). Never part of the shipped repo.
test-samples/
temp-3d-models/
